- Fundoscopic exam (to assess for papilledema — hallmark of increased ICP — or optic atrophy, pallor, or drusen);  
- Visual acuity and confrontation visual fields (to detect central, altitudinal, or bitemporal defects — e.g., chiasmal compression);  
- Pupillary light reflexes (to check for afferent pupillary defect, suggesting optic nerve pathology);  
- Extraocular movements and fundus appearance.  
These are essential to localize the lesion (e.g., optic nerve vs. chiasm vs. post-chiasmal) and guide next testing.

[TEST] Perform fundoscopic examination and document presence/absence of papilledema, optic disc pallor, or drusen. Also assess visual acuity (Snellen chart), confrontation visual fields (superior/inferior/temporal/nasal quadrants), and pupillary light reflexes (including swinging flashlight test for afferent defect).

[THOUGHT] CT is also unavailable. With imaging and advanced testing inaccessible, I must return to domain #7 — physical examination — and ensure *all* critical neuro-ophthalmic elements are explicitly assessed and documented, as this is the only remaining source of objective data. The prior reply mentioned visual acuities (20/120 OD, 20/80 OS) but omitted pupillary responses, fundoscopic findings, extraocular movements, and optic disc appearance — all essential. Since “Not specified” implies those were not reported, I must now request a complete, structured neuro-ophthalmic exam — including direct observation of optic disc morphology (papilledema vs. atrophy), color vision (Ishihara plates), and ocular motility — to detect subtle signs like optic nerve pallor, dysconjugate gaze, or nystagmus that could localize pathology.

[TEST] Comprehensive neuro-ophthalmic examination:  
- Direct ophthalmoscopy with documentation of optic disc color, margins, cup-to-disc ratio, and presence of swelling or pallor;  
- Ishihara color vision testing for each eye separately;  
- Assessment of extraocular movements (full range, smooth pursuit, saccades) and presence of nystagmus or diplopia;  
- Corneal reflexes (to assess trigeminal and facial nerve integrity, given proximity to cavernous sinus/suprasellar region).

